{"sessionId":"dae0915d-49e4-4cf3-83a2-074748d9341b","timestamp":1774600841714,"cwd":"/home/runner/work/CopilotDisprzLearning/CopilotDisprzLearning","prompt":"\n----\n*This section includes details on the pull request with code changes you have already submitted to fix the problem above.*\n\n<pr_title>\nchore: add copilot hooks and lifecycle scripts\n</pr_title>\n\n<pr_description>\n## Summary\r\n- add workspace Copilot hook configuration in .github/hooks/lifecycle-hooks.json\r\n- add Bash and PowerShell lifecycle scripts under scripts/\r\n- add logs/.gitkeep placeholder\r\n- document script flow with inline comments for clarity\n</pr_description>\n\n## Comments on the PR (you are @copilot in this section)\n\n<comments>\n\n<pr_comments>\n\n<comment_old>\n<author>@copilot-pull-request-reviewer</author>\n## Pull request overview\n\nAdds GitHub Copilot workspace lifecycle hook configuration plus cross-platform (Bash/PowerShell) scripts to log Copilot session events, prompts, tool usage, and tool results into a local `logs/` directory for auditing/troubleshooting.\n\n**Changes:**\n- Introduces `.github/hooks/lifecycle-hooks.json` to wire Copilot lifecycle events to local scripts (bash + PowerShell).\n- Adds `scripts/` hook handlers for session start/stop, prompt submission logging, tool-use logging, tool-result logging, and a pre-tool security gate.\n- Adds `logs/.gitkeep` to ensure the logging directory exists in the repo.\n\n### Reviewed changes\n\nCopilot reviewed 13 out of 14 changed files in this pull request and generated 4 comments.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</comment_old>\n\n</pr_comments>\n\n<pr_reviews>\n\n<comment_thread>\n<file>scripts/session-start.sh:7-8</file>\n<original_commit>14a0a21</original_commit>\n\n<comment_old>\n<author>@copilot-pull-request-reviewer</author>\nThese hooks write runtime artifacts into `logs/` under the repo root, which will leave developers with a dirty working tree and creates a real risk of accidentally committing prompt/tool payloads. Consider either (1) writing logs to a location outside the repo (e.g., OS temp / user state dir) or (2) adding `logs/*.log`, `logs/*.jsonl`, etc. to `.gitignore` while keeping `logs/.gitkeep` tracked.\n```suggestion\n# Build absolute path to logs directory outside the git working tree.\nREPO_NAME=\"$(basename \"$ROOT_DIR\")\"\nLOG_DIR=\"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/${REPO_NAME}-logs\"\n```\n</comment_old>\n\n</comment_thread>\n\n<comment_thread>\n<file>scripts/log-tool-result.ps1:15-17</file>\n<original_commit>14a0a21</original_commit>\n\n<comment_old>\n<author>@copilot-pull-request-reviewer</author>\n`tool-results.jsonl` implies JSONL (one JSON value per line), but `Add-Content -Value $Payload` will write multi-line payloads as multiple lines, breaking JSONL parsing. Also consider specifying `-Encoding` explicitly (Windows PowerShell defaults can be non-UTF8) to keep logs consistent across environments.\n```suggestion\n  Add-Content -Path $LogFile -Value \"{}\" -Encoding UTF8\n} else {\n  try {\n    $parsed = $Payload | ConvertFrom-Json\n    $singleLine = $parsed | ConvertTo-Json -Compress\n  } catch {\n    # Fallback: collapse line breaks to keep each record on a single line.\n    $singleLine = ($Payload -replace \"(`r`n|`n|`r)\", \" \")\n  }\n\n  Add-Content -Path $LogFile -Value $singleLine -Encoding UTF8\n```\n</comment_old>\n\n</comment_thread>\n\n<comment_thread>\n<file>.github/hooks/lifecycle-hooks.json:6-8</file>\n<original_commit>14a0a21</original_commit>\n\n<comment_old>\n<author>@copilot-pull-request-reviewer</author>\nThe Windows hook command uses `powershell` without `-NoProfile` / `-NonInteractive`. Profiles can slow execution or introduce side effects/prompts that break hook automation. Consider adding `-NoProfile -NonInteractive` (and optionally using `pwsh` if you intend PowerShell 7+) for more reliable, deterministic hook runs.\n</comment_old>\n\n<comment_new>\n<comment_id>2999682709</comment_id>\n<author>@nilesh-simform</author>\n@copilot apply changes based on <a href=\"https://github.com/nilesh-simform/CopilotDisprzLearning/pull/2#discussion_r2999682709\">this feedback</a>\n</comment_new>\n\n</comment_thread>\n\n<comment_thread>\n<file>scripts/log-tool-result.sh:17-18</file>\n<original_commit>14a0a21</original_commit>\n\n<comment_old>\n<author>@copilot-pull-request-reviewer</author>\nThis file is named `tool-results.jsonl`, but the script appends the raw payload verbatim. If the hook payload contains newlines (e.g., pretty-printed JSON), it will write multiple lines and break the JSONL invariant (1 JSON value per line). Consider compacting/escaping to a single line before writing (or rename the file if multiline entries are expected).\n```suggestion\n  # Ensure payload is a single line to preserve JSONL invariant.\n  if command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n    # Try to compact JSON payload to a single line.\n    if COMPACT_PAYLOAD=\"$(printf '%s' \"$PAYLOAD\" | jq -c . 2>/dev/null)\"; then\n      :\n    else\n      # Fallback: replace newlines with spaces if payload is not valid JSON.\n      COMPACT_PAYLOAD=\"$(printf '%s' \"$PAYLOAD\" | tr '\\n' ' ')\"\n    fi\n  else\n    # Fallback when jq is not available: replace newlines with spaces.\n    COMPACT_PAYLOAD=\"$(printf '%s' \"$PAYLOAD\" | tr '\\n' ' ')\"\n  fi\n\n  # Write compacted payload when available.\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$COMPACT_PAYLOAD\" >> \"$LOG_DIR/tool-results.jsonl\"\n```\n</comment_old>\n\n</comment_thread>\n\n</pr_reviews>\n\n</comments>\n\n----\nThe last **1** git commits in this branch are the changes you have made so far. Use those as your change commit history."}
